-include: ../../../bundle-parent/annotations.bnd
Import-Package: ${annotation-noimports}, *
Bundle-SymbolicName: io.nxmatic.rke2lab.incus.contract.host
Export-Package: io.nxmatic.rke2lab.incus.contract.host
# type=dual-realm — the incus domain's dual-realm host face: what BOTH realms genuinely consume. The
# flat value views the pure-host GROW fetches (InstanceGrowPlan + its sub-views), AND the pure path
# logic both realms share (BootstrapPaths: the provisioning topology the scion materialises into and
# the host derives the instance mounts from). Like seed-broker-codec it is staged as a bundle OSGi-
# side AND kept flat host-side, so its exported package legitimately lives in two realms and
# DUPLICATE_REALM_CLASS exempts it. NOT a seam surface between DOMAINS: producer (incus scion) and
# consumer (incus GROW) are the SAME domain — orthogonal to the contract-purity guard.
#
# GOVERNANCE (the dual-realm library rule, widened 2026-07-17): the red line is NOT "no logic" — it
# is "no dependency absent from either realm". A type may carry pure logic here IFF it depends only
# on the JDK + jspecify (no OSGi type, no other domain, no service reference) and is owned by this
# one domain. This is NOT a new prose rule — it is the EXISTING REALM_BOUNDARY staging law: a class
# may reference only types reachable in its own realm, and a type=dual-realm is flat in BOTH, so its
# visible set is the realm intersection. REALM_BOUNDARY reads method bodies via ASM and breaks the
# build on any forbidden reference — so BootstrapPaths living here is machine-enforced, not just
# documented. BootstrapPaths qualifies: pure java.nio.file.Path derivation, incus-owned, consumed on
# both sides (the scion picks its slot, the host derives the 17 mounts) — so the plan needs no device
# view. It carries no @Component, so it does NOT Require the SCR extender.
Provide-Capability: io.nxmatic.rke2lab.embed; type=dual-realm
